e reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum — i.e., higher sum. So A has no outcome differences (only one outcome), so its pairwise differences sum to zero (only one pair: 1000 - 3000 and 1000 - (-3000)? But that’s not how it's defined). Alternatively, the sum of pairwise differences may refer to the sum over all pairs of (B_outcome - A_outcome). Since A has one outcome and B has two, the pairs are: (3000 - 1000) and (-3000 - 1000). Sum = 2000 - 4000 = -2000. So B has a negative sum. A has no such differences — but if we compute differences from A to B, it would be (1000 - 3000) + (1000 - (-3000)) = -2000 + 4000 = 2000. So the sum of outcome differences from A to B is 2000. The reason says 'the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the lotteries' — this is ambiguous, but likely means the sum over all outcome pairs of (outcome in one lottery minus outcome in the other), and it prefers the one with a more favorable (positive) sum. Since the sum from A to B is 2000 and from B to A is -2000, the more favorable sum is 2000, which corresponds to A. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer A. The information is derivable from the lotteries' outcomes and probabilities. Thus, the decision reason can be applied.",
